.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
#include \(dqbashclient.h\(dq

typedef struct bc_pty_info {
    int rows;        /* actual terminal rows */
    int cols;        /* actual terminal columns */
    int pid;         /* child process PID on server */
    int strip_ansi;  /* 1 if ANSI stripping is active */
} bc_pty_info_t;

int bc_pty_spawn(bc_client_t *c, int rows, int cols,
                 const char *shell, int strip_ansi,
                 bc_pty_info_t *info);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Spawns a new PTY (pseudo\-terminal) session on the bash\-server.
The server calls \f[CR]forkpty(3)\f[R] to create a child process running
the specified shell, with the terminal dimensions set to \f[B]rows\f[R]
x \f[B]cols\f[R].
.PP
The PTY channel (\f[CR]CHAN_PTY\f[R], channel 5) supports full
interactive terminal I/O.
Once spawned, the client can write input with
\f[CR]bc_pty_write(3)\f[R], receive output via
\f[CR]bc_pty_on_output(3)\f[R], resize the terminal with
\f[CR]bc_pty_resize(3)\f[R], send signals with
\f[CR]bc_pty_signal(3)\f[R], and close the session with
\f[CR]bc_pty_close(3)\f[R].
.PP
If \f[B]strip_ansi\f[R] is non\-zero, the server enables an ANSI escape
sequence filter on the PTY output stream.
This removes CSI, OSC, charset designators, and other escape sequences
before delivering output to the client, which is useful for programmatic
consumption of terminal output.
See \f[CR]ansi_strip(3)\f[R] for details on the filter.
.PP
Only one PTY session may be active per client connection at a time.
Calling \f[CR]bc_pty_spawn\f[R] while a PTY is already active returns
\f[CR]BC_ERR_PROTOCOL\f[R].
.SH Wire protocol
Sends on \f[CR]CHAN_PTY\f[R] (channel 5):
.IP
.EX
{\(dqaction\(dq:\(dqspawn\(dq,\(dqrows\(dq:24,\(dqcols\(dq:80,\(dqshell\(dq:\(dq/bin/bash\(dq,\(dqstrip_ansi\(dq:0}
.EE
.PP
Receives on \f[CR]CHAN_PTY\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
{\(dqtype\(dq:\(dqspawned\(dq,\(dqrows\(dq:24,\(dqcols\(dq:80,\(dqpid\(dq:12345,\(dqstrip_ansi\(dq:0}
.EE
